finding: Medium: diagnostics/box3d-parity/records.ts:68 does not identify the first differing record. It only concatenates native's complete step followed by Shallot's complete step, without comparing, record indices, a mismatch summary, or failure status. Thus the advertised recovery command does not make a red name its cause in one run: Hull step 0 prints 8,007 lines / 3,569,824 bytes (independently executed), and a Height Field step contains 14,278 records per side. The oracle's red identifies only the whole step's unequal digests; finding a changed field still requires a separate manual extraction/diff of these large blocks. This fails stage 9's diagnosis claim and the allocated requirement that the command show the first differing record. Keep full-record output if useful, but compare the sides and name the first unequal record with both values (and handle missing records). Finer gold granularity is not necessary if recovery performs that comparison; per-record 16-hex-plus-newline golds would cost 485,452 bytes for Height Field, exceeding the 100 KB cap, and 68,034 bytes for Hull. Independently checked: read unit at kex HEAD, AGENTS.md and full CONTRIBUTING.md; reviewed all stage TypeScript changes and gold-file scope against origin/main. Only the three intended golds changed. Recomputed each digest from its full HEAD^ gold: byte-for-byte equality. Own in-memory mutations changed all six event channels and world hash at Sensor step 68, each of eight cast fields in Height Field's last record, and all 49 area/vertex fields of Hull's last record; all detected in the appropriate step. Digest tests (4), query oracles (4), Sensor public oracle (1) and Sensor native oracle (1) pass against Box3D reference checkout. Existing event field order and raw-f32 query serialization are retained; unexpected non-sensor events explicitly fail. Reader code has no host branch. No repository edits; working tree clean. Cross-host execution and collision resistance were not qualified.
